# HakiOS fragment: the Samsung Exynos 9610 (S5E9610) -- what is true of the SoC, whatever phone it is in.
# Four Cortex-A53s and four Cortex-A73s, all ARMv8.0: no core has PAN (the kernel's own check turns it off,
# xnu/patches/1509) or the ARMv8.2 userspace set (isa_floor=1 on the command line tells user space the 8.0 set,
# xnu/patches/1510). The interrupt controller is a GIC-400 (GICv2: distributor 0x12301000, CPU interface 0x12302000,
# the phone's device tree), which osfmk/arm64/gic_v3_irq.c drives when the loader says so. None of the common
# repository's SoC drivers is this SoC's: they are Qualcomm's; the Exynos ones are the S9's, written for the 9810.
CONFIG_SOC="exynos9610"
# Two cores, A53s 2 and 3 (cpus=2 bootcpu=2, the layout the S9 runs with): S-Boot may park core 1 before the
# hand-over (parked_cores in the loader's platform), and nothing of the A73 cluster has been tried.
CONFIG_CPUS=2
# The kernel on core 2, the loader watching from core 0 (log_other_cpu): a kernel that hangs has its log written
# into Samsung's pstore RAM before the phone restarts.
CONFIG_BOOTCPU=2
# CONFIG_CPUFREQ is not set
# CONFIG_SOCINFO is not set
# CONFIG_THERMAL is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_CPUFREQ is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_SOCINFO is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_THERMAL is not set
# The SoC's temperatures, the TMU: the S9's driver (drivers/power/hakios.exynos_tmu.c) is written for the 9810's;
# this SoC's is at another address (not yet looked at). Off.
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_EXYNOS_TMU is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_PMIC is not set
